. * Output Tables：Overidentification + Sensitivity
. esttab overid sens_full sens_drop sens_drop10 sens_drop10low using SSIV_table1.rtf, ///
>         replace title("Table S9. Shift-share IV diagnostics: Overidentification and sensitivity analysis.") ///
>         mtitles("Over-identification" "Full sample" "Excl. extreme 5%" "Excl. top 10%" "Excl. bottom 10%") ///
>         stats(year county sample N j jp, ///
>                 labels("Year-month FE" "County FE" "Sample restriction" "Observations" "Hansen J statistic" "Hansen J 
> p-value") ///
>                 fmt(0 0 0 0 4 4)) ///
>         star(* 0.10 ** 0.05 *** 0.01) nonotes nocons b(4) se compress nogaps ///
>         order(PI Temp Wind Pop Duration Carbon Water Green Farm Grass) ///
>         note("Notes: This table reports the shift-share IV diagnostics following Goldsmith-Pinkham et al. (70). The in
> strument is Ln(Sunshine) / city CPU, where Ln(Sunshine) is the time-invariant share (natural logarithm of average suns
> hine duration for each county during the period from 1984 to 2013) and 1 / city CPU is the time-varying shift (inverse
>  of city-level climate policy uncertainty index). Column (1) reports the overidentification test. We divide observatio
> ns into terciles based on the CPU level and interact each tercile indicator with the original instrument Ln(Sunshine) 
> / city CPU, yielding three instruments that exploit the same source of identifying variation — the within-city tempora
> l fluctuation of climate policy uncertainty — but operate under different policy uncertainty regimes (low, medium, and
>  high CPU). We estimate the overidentified model using ivreghdfe with cluster-robust standard errors, which automatica
> lly absorbs county and year-month fixed effects and reports the Hansen J statistic. The Hansen J statistic tests wheth
> er all instruments are jointly valid (H0: all instruments are valid; failure to reject supports instrument validity). 
> Columns (2)-(5) present the sensitivity analysis: Column (2) uses the full sample; Column (3) excludes counties in the
>  top and bottom 5% of the Ln(Sunshine) distribution; Column (4) excludes the top 10%; Column (5) excludes the bottom 1
> 0%. The dependent variable is ShannonBD (the negative sum of the proportion of each bird species multiplied by its nat
> ural logarithm). The endogenous variable is PSI (the weighted sum of policies at the provincial, municipal, and county
>  levels), instrumented by Ln(Sunshine) / city CPU. Other control variables include Temp (annual average temperature), 
> Wind (annual average wind speed), Pop (the logarithm of the ratio of total population to regional area), Duration (the
>  logarithm of the ratio of birdwatching duration to the number of birdwatchers), Carbon (the logarithm of the carbon e
> missions), Water (the area of water divided by the total area), Green (the sum of the area of forests and shrubs divid
> ed by the total area), Farm (the area of farmland divided by the total area), Grass (the area of the grassland divided
>  by the total area). All specifications include county and year-month fixed effects. The robust standard errors cluste
> red by county are reported in parentheses. ***, **, * denote significance at the 1%, 5%, and 10% levels, respectively.
> ")
(output written to SSIV_table1.rtf)

. * Output Tables：Balance test + Shift-based IV identification tests 
. esttab balance shift_s1a shift_s2a shift_s1b shift_s2b using SSIV_table2.rtf, ///
>         replace title("Table S10. Share exogeneity and shift-based IV identification tests.") ///
>         mtitles("Share exogeneity" "First stage" "Second stage" "First stage" "Second stage") ///
>         mgroups("Balance test" "Panel A: [Ln(Sunshine) / city CPU] AND [Ln(Sunshine) x Year-month FE]" "Panel B: 1 / c
> ity CPU only", pattern(1 1 0 1 0)) ///
>         stats(N r2 F yearfe countyfe sun_year, ///
>         labels("Observations" "R-squared" "F-statistic" "Year-month FE" "County FE" "Ln(Sunshine) x Year-month FE") //
> /
>         fmt(0 4 2 0 0 0)) ///
>         star(* 0.10 ** 0.05 *** 0.01) nonotes nocons b(4) se(4) compress nogaps ///
>         order(Sun_ccpu inv_CCPU PI $control) ///
>         keep(Sun_ccpu inv_CCPU PI $control) ///
>         note("Notes: This table reports the share exogeneity test and the shift-based IV identification results. Colum
> n (1) tests whether the time-invariant share component Ln(Sunshine) is correlated with observable county characteristi
> cs following Goldsmith-Pinkham et al. (70). The dependent variable in Column (1) is Ln(Sunshine), natural logarithm of
>  average sunshine duration from 1984 to 2013. The data is collapsed to county-level means. If the share is exogenous, 
> it should be uncorrelated with county-level covariates. Note that in the main IV specification, county fixed effects f
> ully absorb the time-invariant cross-sectional variation in Ln(Sunshine), so any correlations observed here do not thr
> eaten identification. Columns (2)-(5) report the shift-based IV identification results following Borusyak et al. (69).
>  Columns (2)-(3) use the original instrument Ln(Sunshine)/CPU but additionally control for Ln(Sunshine) x Year-month F
> E, so that any time-varying effect of sunshine operating through channels other than climate policy is fully absorbed;
>  identification relies exclusively on within-city temporal variation in CPU. Columns (4)-(5) use a pure shift instrume
> nt 1/CPU, which does not involve the sunshine share at all. Columns (2) and (4) report first-stage regressions where t
> he dependent variable is PSI (the weighted sum of policies at the provincial, municipal, and county levels). Columns (
> 3) and (5) report second-stage regressions where the dependent variable is ShannonBD (the negative sum of the proporti
> on of each bird species multiplied by its natural logarithm). The slight variation in sample sizes across specificatio
> ns reflects differences in the availability of the CPU index and sunshine data across county-year observations. Contro
> l variables include Temp (annual average temperature), Wind (annual average wind speed), Pop (the logarithm of the rat
> io of total population to regional area), Duration (the logarithm of the ratio of birdwatching duration to the number 
> of birdwatchers), Carbon (the logarithm of carbon emissions), Water (the area of water divided by the total area), Gre
> en (the sum of the area of forests and shrubs divided by the total area), Farm (the area of farmland divided by the to
> tal area), Grass (the area of the grassland divided by the total area). Columns (2)-(5) include county and year-month 
> fixed effects with robust standard errors clustered by county. Column (1) reports robust standard errors. ***, **, * d
> enote significance at the 1%, 5%, and 10% levels, respectively.")
